  CaseProcessing.Tests rose from 312 to 340: 28 new PayloadStructureGuardTests.
Acceptance criteria: T02 mechanical acceptance met.
  - AC-11 (null Boxes), AC-12 (a null element), AC-13 (null, empty, and
    whitespace-only Code) each pass at unit level against
    PayloadStructureGuard.Inspect.
  - The guard provably reads no T4RawBox.Value: values do not change the result
    (identical structure with planted values yields equal errors), planted values
    reach no Field or Message, and an executable-line source scan finds no
    `.Value` read - with a scanner guard so a wrong path or an over-eager comment
    filter cannot make the scan pass for free.
  - One error per structural defect, in fixed order; a null Boxes collection
    short-circuits, so only one error is possible there.
  - Never throws and never returns null for any payload shape, including a null
    payload and a null Boxes collection.
  - Every emitted Field is a T4ProcessingFields constant, and only the two
    structural ones (PayloadBoxes, PayloadBoxCode) are ever used.
  - Messages are non-interpolated constants: six defective boxes with six
    different values still yield exactly one distinct message per rule.
  - The payload is unmutated and reference-identical afterwards - same collection
    reference, same element references in the same order, untrimmed codes, all
    identity fields unchanged. Repeated calls are equal in order.
  - Structural only: well-formed, empty, duplicate-code, unknown-code,
    blank-valued, and garbage-valued payloads all yield zero errors, so
    duplicates preserved by spec 007 still reach the parser and the parser's own
    duplicate rejection fires.
Decision flagged for one-pass human override:
  [ASSUMED] Error order is rule-major then positional - every null-element defect
  first, then every blank-code defect. architecture.md fixes the rule order but
  not whether the two per-box rules interleave positionally. Recorded in the XML
  doc on Inspect and asserted by
  Inspect_SeveralDefectsAtOnce_YieldsOneErrorEachInFixedOrder.
Mutation evidence (the new arms are not vacuous):
  - Append box.Value to the blank-code message -> 4 tests fail
    (Inspect_ValuesDoNotAffectTheResult, GuardSource_ReadsNoBoxValue,
    Inspect_PlantedValuesEverywhere_AppearInNoErrorFieldOrMessage,
    Inspect_MessagesAreNonInterpolatedConstants).
  - Remove the null-element rule -> 4 tests fail.
  - Remove the null-Boxes short-circuit -> 7 tests fail, including both
    never-throws arms.
  - Silently dedupe/trim/drop boxes before reporting -> 4 tests fail.
  - T4RawInput.Boxes is init-only, so a mutation that reassigns the caller's
    collection does not compile (CS8852): payload immutability is partly
    compiler-enforced, not only test-enforced.
